INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('city-energy-lab-symposium', 'City Energy Lab Symposium', '/city-energy-lab-symposium/', 'Schedule 10:30am-10:40am Introduction of the Workshop and the team (Steven) 10:40am-11:10am Shape grammars and Shape Machine (Thanos) 11:10am - 12:30pm Interactive Introduction to Shape Machine (Thanos and Kurt) 12:30pm - 1:30pm Lunch 1:30pm - 2:00pm Shape Machine and Shape Signature (James) 2:00pm - 2:30pm Shape Machine and Interpreters (Kurt) 2:30pm - 3:00pm Representation and Process in Smart Design (Steven) 3:00pm - 3:15pm Coffee break Abstracts Shape grammars and Shape Machine A brief sketch of the shape grammar formalism, one of the most powerful formal systems for the generative description of designs, is given. The talk discusses the foundations of the shape grammar formalism in terms of algebras of shapes, labeled shapes, weighted shapes and shape rules defined over these algebras and provides a useful overview of its applications in design research. The emphasis is given in current research work at Georgia Institute of Technology and in particular the Shape Machine, an in-house software currently in development in the Shape Computation Lab, that promises to become the par excellent tool for formal studies in visual analysis and design and design research at large. Interactive introduction to Shape Machine An interactive survey of the expressive power of Shape Machine, a new shape grammar interpreter, is presented. The work is presented in two parts: A brief presentation of a series of shape computations that have been routinely used as benchmarks for the design tasks a shape grammar interpreter should be able to accomplish; and a brief exploration of design applications in various domains, namely, product modeling, mechanical and architectural design to suggest possibilities for new design workflows and/or new trajectories in new domains too. Some speculations on the future of the technology pertaining to its potential usage in a new paradigm of programming with shapes (programming by drawing) and/or the envision of a new paradigm of a physical computer are presented in the end. Shape Machine and Shape Signature A brief outline of the mathematical representation of shapes in Shape Machine is discussed. The dual representation of shapes in terms of carrier lines and maximal lines is given in the form of a unique shape signature that is used in an enumeration to count all possible shapes up to four lines. The possible extensions of the shape signature formalism to provide a unique parametric signature of shapes in 2D and 3D parametric modeling software is given in the end. Shape Machine and Interpreters A brief sketch of shape grammar interpreters is given structured around the two key characteristics of the shape grammar formalism: recursion and fusion (embedding), and the ways both have been implemented in software applications. A special emphasis is given to general purpose interpreters and mostly, on the ways the embedding relation, the most uncanny and evancenscent quality of shape computation, has been implemented. The talk builds on current classifications of shape grammar interpreters in the literature but significantly extends them to include issues pertaining to shape types, domains of transformations, performance, computational precision and system sensitivity. Shape Machine Workshop An interactive hands-on discussion with the audience brainstorming on current and future design workflows in multiple disciplines including Architecture, Industrial Design, Graphic Design, Mechanical Engineering and Computer Science. The audience will be invited to propose current workflows in design practice that will be visually modelled on the spot to explore the expressive power of the Shape machine to quickly and seamlessly integrate such patterns and workflows in design inquiry. Blurbs Athanassios Economou, Dipl. Arch, M.Arch, PhD Professor of Architecture thanos@gatech.edu Athanassios (Thanos) Economou is Professor in the College of Design at the Georgia Institute of Technology. Dr. Economou''s teaching and research are in');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('city-energy-lab-welcomes-everyone-from-gses-to-join-a-guest-lecture', 'City Energy Lab welcomes everyone from GSES to join a guest lecture.', '/city-energy-lab-welcomes-everyone-from-gses-to-join-a-guest-lecture/', '[City Energy Lab Lecture] City Energy Lab welcomes everyone from GSES to join a guest lecture. Speaker: Prof. Xinyue Ye from Texas A&M University-College Station (TAMU) Topic: Urban Artificial Intelligence: Practice and Prospects Time: October 17th, 2022 11:00 AM - 12:00 PM Venue: Zoom Lecture Short Bio: Prof. Xinyue Ye is Fellow of American Association of Geographers (AAG) and Fellow of Royal Geographical Society (with IBG), holding Harold L. Adams Endowed Professorship in Department of Landscape Architecture and Urban Planning & Department of Geography at Texas A&M University-College Station (TAMU), USA. He directs the focus of transportation in the PhD program of Urban and Regional Science at TAMU, and is the Interim Director of Center for Housing and Urban Development. His research focuses on geospatial artificial intelligence, geographic information system, and smart cities. Prof. Ye won the national first-place research award from University Economic Development Association. He was the recipient of annual research awards from both computational science (New Jersey Institute of Technology) and Geography (Kent State University) as well as AAG Regional Development and Planning Distinguished Scholar Award. He was one of the top 10 young scientists named by The World Geospatial Developers Conference in 2021. His work has been funded by National Science Foundation, National Institute of Justice, Department of Commerce, Department of Energy, and Department of Transportation. Prof. Ye is Editor-in-Chief of Computational Urban Science, an open access journal published by Springer. He also serves as the co-editor of Journal of Planning Education and Research, the flagship journal of Association of Collegiate Schools of Planning. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
