INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-a-machine-learning-based-mediation-analysis-of-urban-form-influences-on-building-energy-use-evidence-from-seoul-south-korea', '[Publication] A Machine Learning-Based Mediation Analysis of Urban Form Influences on Building Energy Use: Evidence from Seoul, South Korea', '/publication-a-machine-learning-based-mediation-analysis-of-urban-form-influences-on-building-energy-use-evidence-from-seoul-south-korea/', '[Publication Details] • Journal: Sustainable Cities and Society (2026) • Authors: Parth Bansal and Steven Jige Quan • Article Number: 107353 • DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scs.2026.107353 Abstract Understanding the mechanisms through which urban contextual form influences building energy use is critical for developing accurate urban building energy models. However, few studies have examined the mechanisms that mediate the effect of urban form on building energy use. This study investigates three mediating mechanisms that link urban form to building energy use: solar insolation, contextual temperature, and occupant behavior. Using monthly energy use of residential plots in Seoul, South Korea, the study estimates mediation effects in summer and winter seasons. An artificial neural network (ANN) mediation model is trained to capture nonlinearity and interactions in the relationship and compared to traditional linear mediation models. Results suggest the ANN-based method has a higher accuracy and enhanced interpretability. Analyses show that contextual temperature is the dominant mediator for summer electricity and winter energy uses, with effects of 24-54%, while solar insolation also plays a major mediating role, with effects up to 47% in winter electricity. Occupant behavior mediates approximately 2-5% of urban form''s effects on seasonal energy uses. Although modest, these effects are significant for green and water cover. Notably, significant residual effects persist for building height, and distances to green and water, especially for winter gas, indicating additional, unmodeled pathways. Partial dependence plots are used to assess the nature of the relationship. Sensitivity analyses for context size, model specifications, regularization, spatial autocorrelation, and sub-contexts are conducted, offering comparative and methodological insights. Overall, the study clarifies how urban form influences energy use, informing urban planning and energy policies. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-analyzing-seasonal-and-nonlinear-built-environment-energy-dynamics-using-street-view-imagery-in-new-york-city', '[Publication] Analyzing seasonal and nonlinear built environment-energy dynamics using street view imagery in New York City', '/publication-analyzing-seasonal-and-nonlinear-built-environment-energy-dynamics-using-street-view-imagery-in-new-york-city/', 'Qi Lin, Yichun Zhou, Steven Jige Quan, ChengHe Guan has published an article in Sustainable Cities and Society . Highlight This study develops a novel framework using street view imagery to reveal how built environment–energy relationships vary nonlinearly across seasons. It highlights the importance of incorporating human-scale visual data and seasonality into urban energy modeling. [Publication Details] • Journal: Sustainable Cities and Society (2026) • Authors: Qi Lin, Yichun Zhou, Steven Jige Quan, ChengHe Guan • DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scs.2026.107325 Abstract This study develops a community level framework integrating street view imagery (SVI) and semantic segmentation to quantify built-environment indicators and relate them to building energy use intensity, explicitly accounting for seasonality. Using over 48,000 Google Street view images from summer and winter 2017 in New York City, per-image shares of urban features are computed, aggregated to community units, and benchmarked against conventional urban-form measures, thereby providing a human‑view, design‑legible complement. Electricity and gas intensities by sector are modeled for both seasons with linear regression and Random Forest. The findings reveal that aggregated SVI indicators align strongly with conventional measures under summer imagery, with associations weakening in winter. The relationships between SVI-derived built-environment indicators and energy intensities are nonlinear and heterogeneous across sectors, energy types, and seasons. The SVI-derived indicators provide a human-view proxy that bridges design visualization and community level energy modeling. We conclude that seasonality substantially conditions SVI-energy relationships and should be explicitly controlled during SVI collection, while nonlinearity is a defining feature of built environment-energy linkages. The proposed framework is low‑cost, scalable, and transferable to cities with comparable data infrastructure, supporting scenario evaluation and planning strategies for energy efficiency and emissions reduction. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-generative-artificial-intelligence-in-urban-planning-and-design-current-explorations-and-future-trends', '[Publication] Generative Artificial Intelligence in Urban Planning and Design: Current Explorations and Future Trends', '/publication-generative-artificial-intelligence-in-urban-planning-and-design-current-explorations-and-future-trends/', 'Steven Jige Qua n has published an article in 환경논총 (Journal of Environmental Studies), Vol. 77 . Highlight This study reviews the emerging role of generative AI across the entire urban planning process and outlines key future trends including multimodal models, agentic AI, and world models. It highlights both transformative potentials and critical challenges for integrating AI into planning practice, research, and education. [Publication Details] • Journal: 환경논총 (Journal of Environmental Studies) Vol. 77 (2026) • Author: Steven Jige Quan • Affiliation: Seoul National University, Graduate School of Environmental Studies Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-hierarchical-reinforcement-learning-for-energy-efficient-urban-planning', '[Publication] Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning for Energy-Efficient Urban Planning', '/publication-hierarchical-reinforcement-learning-for-energy-efficient-urban-planning/', '[Publication Details] • Journal: ACM Transactions on Intelligent Systems and Technology, Volume 17, Issue 5 (2026) • Authors: Y. Xiao, L. Jiang, S. J. Quan, P. Wang, M. Yin, and P. Wang • Pages: 1–26 • DOI: https://doi.org/10.1145/3810947 Abstract Urban planning plays a pivotal role in fostering sustainable, energy-efficient communities amidst the escalating challenges of climate change and energy crises. However, traditional methods often overlook the hierarchical interdependencies inherent in urban structures, land use configurations, and building design, leading to suboptimal outcomes. Here, we introduce a Hierarchical Energy-Efficient Urban Planning (HEEUP) framework, leveraging hierarchical reinforcement learning (HRL) to integrate decision-making across three tiers: urban structure, land use configuration, and building design. Our approach facilitates collaboration between cascading agents, enabling energy-conscious planning decisions that align with functional and contextual urban demands. Evaluations across real-world datasets from Miami and Albuquerque demonstrate a 7.3% improvement in energy efficiency compared to baseline methods. Furthermore, HEEUP achieves an average 24.52% reduction in computational training time compared to traditional optimization algorithms and generative machine learning models. These findings underscore the potential of HEEUP to significantly advance energy-efficient urban planning, providing a scalable, adaptable paradigm for addressing contemporary sustainability challenges. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-hierarchical-reinforcement-learning-for-energy-efficient-urban-planning-2', '[Publication] Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning for Energy-Efficient Urban Planning', '/publication-hierarchical-reinforcement-learning-for-energy-efficient-urban-planning-2/', '[Publication Details] Journal: ACM Transactions on Intelligent Systems and Technology (2026) DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.buildenv.2026.115226 Authors: Yanan Xiao, Lu Jiang, Steven Jige Quan, Pengfei Wang, Minghao Yin, and Pengyang Wang Date: June 2026 Abstract Urban planning plays a pivotal role in fostering sustainable, energy-efficient communities amidst the escalating challenges of climate change and energy crises. However, traditional methods often overlook the hierarchical interdependencies inherent in urban structures, land use configurations, and building design, leading to suboptimal outcomes. Here, we introduce a Hierarchical Energy-Efficient Urban Planning (HEEUP) framework, leveraging hierarchical reinforcement learning (HRL) to integrate decision-making across three tiers: urban structure, land use configuration, and building design. Our approach facilitates collaboration between cascading agents, enabling energy-conscious planning decisions that align with functional and contextual urban demands. Evaluations across real-world datasets from Miami and Albuquerque demonstrate a 7.3% improvement in energy efficiency compared to baseline methods. Furthermore, HEEUP achieves an average 24.52% reduction in computational training time compared to traditional optimization algorithms and generative machine learning models. These findings underscore the potential of HEEUP to significantly advance energy-efficient urban planning, providing a scalable, adaptable paradigm for addressing contemporary sustainability challenges. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-mixture-of-urban-form-archetypes-in-seoul-a-signal-processing-approach', '[Publication] Mixture of urban form archetypes in Seoul: A signal processing approach', '/publication-mixture-of-urban-form-archetypes-in-seoul-a-signal-processing-approach/', '[Publication Details] Journal: URBAN DESIGN International (2026) DOI: https://doi.org/10.1057/s41289-026-00349-z Authors: S. J. Quan and S. Kim Date: Received: 31 January 2026 / Accepted: 22 July 2026 Abstract Seoul’s urban morphology reflects successive phases, from colonial rule through rapid urbanization to regeneration, yet most analyses reduce this complexity to static clusters. This study models urban form as mixtures of latent archetypes, casting the task as blind source separation. SimplexNMF, a simplex constrained non-negative matrix factorization (NMF) algorithm, is applied to 2019 GIS indicators for 1418 500 × 500-m grid cells (buildings, streets, green/water, topography). This study recovers nine interpretable archetypes: organic low-rise residential, low-rise mixed-use, low-density foothill or interchange edge, linear mid-rise, riverside, high-rise apartment, institutional/public-facility, hilly-terrain, and park-edge patterns. Hybrid clustering of their compositional weights further identifies nine archetype-dominant and six mixed clusters. Comparison with knowledge-based typologies and analysis of two cases (Yeongdong 2 District-a, Jamsil) help interpret these mixtures and demonstrate the complementary roles of data-driven and expert-based approaches, highlighting how plan ideals, local adaptations, and path dependence shaped the evolution of their urban forms. By translating overlapping forms into compo sitional signals, the framework offers a scalable, theory-informed basis for diagnosing neighborhood character and guiding context-sensitive regeneration in Seoul and similarly layered cities. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-multi-objective-design-optimization-of-educational-buildings-using-catboost-and-nsga-iii-under-future-climate-scenarios', '[Publication] Multi-objective design optimization of educational buildings using CatBoost and NSGA-III under future climate scenarios', '/publication-multi-objective-design-optimization-of-educational-buildings-using-catboost-and-nsga-iii-under-future-climate-scenarios/', '[Publication Details] Journal: Energy (2026) DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.energy.2026.141386 Authors: Yeqin Shen, Steven Jige Quan, Yingnan Li, Waishan Qiu, Yuankai Wang, Junga Lee Date: September 2026 Abstract In the context of accelerating climate change and rising energy challenges, enhancing the adaptability and sustainability of educational buildings is crucial. This study proposes a framework integrating multi-source data, climate scenario simulations, surrogate modeling, and multi-objective optimization to overcome the limitations of conventional building performance optimization approaches in handling multiple building types, future climate scenarios, and mixed design variables. Hourly weather data for 2050 and 2080 under four CMIP6-based SSP scenarios (SSP1-2.6, SSP2-4.5, SSP3-7.0, SSP5-8.5) were used to simulate the thermal, daylighting, and energy performance of three typical school building types (I-, L-, and C-shaped) in a hot-summer and cold-winter climate. Six performance indicators were considered, including daylight performance metrics, building energy use intensity, and thermal comfort assessment indices. The CatBoost-based surrogate model accelerated simulations by approximately 180 times and achieved higher accuracy with discrete variables. Integrated training outperformed individual models and enabled the simultaneous simulation of different building types. In multi-scenario optimization, fixing gene positions for climate scenarios and building typologies within the NSGA chromosome structure allowed the model to better capture cross-scenario interactions and accelerate convergence. Window-to-wall ratio, orientation, shading configuration, and wall material were key determinants of energy and thermal adaptability, while greater room depth improved comfort and reduced energy use. Optimal solutions favored south-southeast orientations and thicker exterior walls. This research provides a scalable, interpretable, and adaptive method for sustainable building design under climate uncertainty. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-nonlinear-relationship-between-urban-form-and-heat-resilience-an-empirical-study-of-seoul', '[Publication] Nonlinear relationship between urban form and heat resilience: An empirical study of Seoul', '/publication-nonlinear-relationship-between-urban-form-and-heat-resilience-an-empirical-study-of-seoul/', '[Publication Details] Journal: Building and Environment (2026) DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.buildenv.2026.115226 Authors: Jianqi Li, Lili Lin, Danbi Kim, Chaosu Li, Wei Wang, and Steven Jige Quan Date: 30 April 2026 / Accepted: 7 September 2026 Abstract As heatwaves intensify under climate change, research has increasingly shifted from identifying urban heat island effects to understanding cities'' capacity to withstand extreme heat, conceptualized as urban heat resilience (HR). Although previous studies have demonstrated that urban form influences spatial heat patterns, it remains unclear which urban form characteristics enhance or reduce HR and whether these relationships are linear, nonlinear, or shaped by spatial dependence. Using Seoul as a case study, we examine the relationship between urban form and a land surface temperature-based proxy of HR in 2016 using a $480\times480$ m grid. We applied a Spatial Durbin Model (SDM), which accounts for spatial dependence and estimates direct and spillover effects, along with gradient boosting decision trees (GBDT), which capture nonlinear relationships. Consistent findings across the two models are interpreted as more robust evidence, whereas discrepancies are considered indicative of potential sensitivity, nonlinear responses, and spatial complexity. Vegetation and water presence were consistently positively associated with HR based on the estimated total effects. The GBDT results further indicated nonlinear associations, with a transition region near an NDVI of approximately 0.07 and a gradual plateau in the association with water presence at a water ratio of approximately 60%. Because many Seoul neighborhoods remain close to these transition regions, targeted expansion of green and blue infrastructure may be a promising intervention for improving HR. In contrast, building coverage shows a consistent negative association with HR in both the GBDT result and the SDM direct-effect estimate, emphasizing the importance of increasing urban openness in Seoul''s dense built environment. In addition, heat resilience patterns vary more across events than temperature patterns. This study demonstrates the value of model triangulation and offers an analytical approach for investigating complex urban climate processes. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO pages (slug, title, url, text) VALUES ('publication-urban-cooling-and-energy-saving-effects-of-nature-based-solutions-across-types-and-scales', '[Publication] Urban Cooling and Energy-Saving Effects of Nature-Based Solutions Across Types and Scales', '/publication-urban-cooling-and-energy-saving-effects-of-nature-based-solutions-across-types-and-scales/', '[Publication Details] • Journal: Nature Cities, Volume 2, Issue 12 (2025) • Authors: H. Wei, X. Bai, Q. Lu, J. Wu, F. Su, T. Hong, et al., and Y. Han • Pages: 1194–1204 Abstract The escalating global demand for urban cooling has increased the need to integrate nature-based solutions (NBS) into urban planning. These strategies help mitigate the urban heat island effect and reduce energy use associated with buildings. Despite growing interest in NBS, comparative evidence on their climate-specific and scale-dependent performance remains limited. We conduct a systematic meta-analysis of 373 peer-reviewed studies (2013–2025) spanning all 16 Köppen–Geiger climate zones. The results reveal that NBS reduce daytime temperatures by 2.04 ± 0.17 °C during hot periods and lower annual cooling loads by 1.32 ± 0.06% globally. Green infrastructure outperforms blue infrastructure in both thermal regulation and energy savings across most climates. The spatial scale also critically shapes outcomes, as neighborhood-scale NBS deliver the strongest cooling effects (−2.22 ± 0.25 °C) while building-scale strategies yield optimal energy savings (8.62 ± 0.78%). These climate- and scale-dependent patterns provide actionable guidance for prioritizing NBS to strengthen urban energy resilience. Categories: Uncategorized 0 Comments Leave a Reply Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Name * Email * Website What''s on your mind? Search for: Recent Posts [Field Trip] 2026-2 Urban Planning and Zoning Class The commencement day of Lili [Conference] Professor Steven Jige Quan at the 7th LH Global Business Convention (WSCE 2026) Professor Quan gave an online lecture on presentation title: "Examining Model Accuracy and Reliability of Explanations in Explainable Machine Learning for Urban Studies: A Case Study of Residential Building Energy Use in Seoul"at the machine learning methods forum Professor Quan delivered a lecture on "Explainable Machine Learning in Urban Research" at the Machine Learning Methods Forum at Chongqing University. Recent Comments ปั้มไลค์ on Joint Seminar with Hanyang University''s Urban Design and Spatial Analysis Lab Archives November 2026 September 2026 August 2026 May 2026 April 2026 March 2026 February 2026 December 2025 September 2025 August 2025 June 2025 February 2025 January 2025 November 2024 October 2024 April 2024 March 2024 January 2024 October 2023 July 2023 May 2023 April 2023 February 2023 November 2022 August 2022 May 2022 April 2022 March 2022 December 2021 October 2021 August 2021 October 2019 July 2019 May 2019 March 2019 June 2017 Categories NEWS Principle Investigator / Publications Uncategorized Meta Log in Entries feed Comments feed WordPress.org');
